Month 5 — Day 10: Docker CMD & Container Startup Behavior

Objective

Understand how Docker CMD defines the default command executed when a container starts, and observe the container lifecycle after the main process finishes.

1. Project Setup

Created a dedicated project directory:

mkdir -p ~/day10-cmd-entrypoint
cd ~/day10-cmd-entrypoint

2. Dockerfile

Created a Dockerfile using the Ubuntu base image and CMD instruction.

FROM ubuntu:latest
CMD ["echo", "Hello Docker - DAY10"]

Concepts Learned

- "FROM" defines the base image used to build a Docker image.
- "CMD" specifies the default command that runs when a container starts.
- The command is executed when Docker starts a container from the image.

3. Building the Docker Image

sudo docker build -t day10-cmd:v1 .

What I Learned

- "docker build" builds a new image using the Dockerfile.
- "-t" assigns a name and tag to the image.
- "." specifies the current directory as the build context.
- Docker automatically pulls the required base image if it is not available locally.

4. Running the Container

sudo docker run --name day10-cmd-test day10-cmd:v1

Output

Hello Docker - DAY10

This confirmed that Docker successfully created the container and executed the command defined in CMD.

5. Container Lifecycle Verification

sudo docker ps -a

Observation

After displaying the message, the container stopped.

What I Learned

- A container runs while its main process is running.
- When the main process finishes execution, the container exits.
- A container in the Exited state is not necessarily an error.
- Containers are designed to run processes, not simply remain active indefinitely.

Key Commands Practiced

Command| Purpose
"docker build"| Build an image from a Dockerfile
"docker run"| Create and start a container
"docker ps -a"| View all containers, including stopped containers

